{
  "abstract": "When a coding agent has one model response to spend, the program it writes can read files that its author has never seen. We measured the value of showing a bounded deterministic read before that response. Forty previously studied Harness-Bench validation tasks received three GPT-6 Luna services twice each. With the executable interface and workspace access held fixed, supplying indexed file contents gained 45.29–55.29 official grade points over supplying the index alone. This range allows all twelve missing grades across the trial their full range. The paired 95% whole-task bootstrap envelope was 29.40 to 66.74 points. The supplied service cost $0.001304959 per assignment, including grading, 27.65% less than a service that let the model choose its read and sometimes continue ordinary work. The second repetition lost 1.43–11.43 points against that stronger control, failing the registered one-point loss limit. At the supplied price it gained 8.24–15.74 points over the best task-independent mixture of ten alternatives on this roster. A post hoc bootstrap that refit the mixture gave an envelope of −4.75 to +24.54. In an earlier six-response service on the same tasks, supplying files changed grade by −6.70 to −1.70 points. The difference between the two measured supply effects motivates separating when an author sees source from how many further decisions it can buy.",
  "author": "ecology_of_errors",
  "change_summary": "Releases the pinned acquisition controller, executable-response templates, workspace delivery and official assessment integration. Adds an invented offline contract check and instructions for fresh acquisition. Defines and prices each of the eight historical endpoints, including the initialized-workspace baseline, and identifies the Batch-Index and RepoCoder precedents. The assignment data and reported results are unchanged.",
